"""The 22 source documents, summarised from UK Food Standards Agency guidance.

Kept in one module so the app has no external data files to deploy.
"""

DOCUMENTS = {
    "allergen_cross_contact.txt": "Allergen cross contact in the kitchen\n\nCross contact happens when an allergen moves from one food to another in amounts too small to see. Unlike bacterial contamination, cooking does not remove it. A trace of the protein is enough to cause a reaction.\n\nCommon routes:\n\nShared utensils, such as using the same tongs for a gluten free roll and a standard one\nShared fryers, where battered fish and chips share oil\nFlour dust in the air settling on open food\nWiping cloths carrying residue between surfaces\nSpoons left in containers and moved between dishes\nCrumbs in a toaster or on a slicing blade\n\nControls that work:\n\nPrepare allergen free orders first, before the surface has been used for other food\nClean and disinfect surfaces and equipment before starting, and wash hands\nUse separate, clearly identified equipment such as colour coded boards and utensils\nStore allergens in sealed containers below other foods so they cannot spill downwards\nKeep ingredient packaging until service is over so the exact product can be checked\n\nA separate preparation area is the strongest control where space allows.\n\nIf an allergen free order cannot be prepared safely, tell the customer rather than taking the risk.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "allergen_info_loose_food.txt": "Allergen information for non-prepacked food\n\nFood that is not prepacked includes anything served on a plate, sold loose from a counter, or packed to order in front of the customer. Most restaurant, pub and takeaway food falls into this category.\n\nFor this food, allergen information must be available, but it does not have to be written on the item itself. It may be given verbally by a member of staff.\n\nIf information is given verbally, there must be clear signposting telling customers where to ask. A notice on the menu, on the counter or on a chalkboard directing customers to speak to a member of staff satisfies this.\n\nStaff who answer allergen questions must have access to accurate ingredient information, usually a written matrix or recipe file kept behind the counter. Answering from memory is where most serious incidents begin.\n\nNever guess, and never say a dish is probably fine. If the information is not available, say so.\n\nRecipes change. A supplier substitution, a different brand of sauce or a new product line can introduce an allergen into a dish that was previously free of it. The ingredient record has to be updated whenever a product changes, which means checking supplier specifications on delivery rather than assuming last month's information still holds.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "allergen_labelling_ppds.txt": "Allergen labelling for prepacked for direct sale food\n\nSince 1 October 2021, food that is prepacked for direct sale, known as PPDS, must carry a label showing the name of the food and a full list of ingredients, with the 14 allergens emphasised within that list. The change is commonly called Natasha's Law.\n\nPPDS food is food that is packaged at the same premises where it is sold, and is in its packaging before the customer orders or selects it. A sandwich made in the morning and placed in a chiller for customers to pick up is PPDS. Examples include packaged sandwiches, salad boxes, bakery items wrapped in advance and pre-wrapped cheese.\n\nFood packed to order at the customer's request is not PPDS, and nor is food sold loose or served on a plate.\n\nEmphasis usually means bold type, but capitals, underlining, a contrasting colour or a different font are all acceptable as long as the allergen stands out from the rest of the ingredients list.\n\nLabels may be handwritten, provided they are easily visible and clear to read.\n\nThe requirements do not apply to distance selling, such as food ordered online for delivery, although allergen information must still be provided by other means.\n\nFailing to comply is a criminal offence.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency, https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/introduction-to-allergen-labelling-changes-ppds",
    "chill_holding.txt": "Chill holding temperatures\n\nIn England, Wales and Northern Ireland, food that could allow harmful bacteria to grow must be held at 8 degrees Celsius or below. This is a legal requirement, not a recommendation.\n\nIn practice most businesses set fridges lower than the legal maximum. The FSA advises setting chillers to 5 degrees or below, because fridge temperatures fluctuate when doors open, when warm stock goes in, and during defrost cycles. A unit set at 8 degrees will regularly drift above the limit.\n\nScotland does not set a single numerical chill temperature in law. Instead the law requires that food is not kept at temperatures that would cause a risk to health, and the business must justify its own controls.\n\nLimited periods outside temperature control are allowed where they are necessary for practical handling during preparation, transport, display and service. These are exceptions for short working periods, not a general allowance.\n\nRefrigeration slows bacterial growth. It does not kill bacteria, and it does not reverse growth that has already happened. Food that has been left out and then returned to the fridge is not made safe again.\n\nHigh risk foods needing close control include cooked meat, cooked rice, dairy products, seafood, sauces, prepared salads and anything ready to eat that will not be cooked again before serving.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "cleaning_and_disinfection.txt": "Cleaning and disinfection\n\nCleaning and disinfection are two different things. Cleaning removes visible dirt and grease. Disinfection reduces bacteria to a safe level. A surface must be cleaned before it can be disinfected, because dirt protects bacteria from the disinfectant.\n\nThe two stage method for food contact surfaces:\n\nRemove loose debris and wash with hot water and detergent\nRinse if the product requires it\nApply disinfectant and leave it for the full contact time stated on the label\nRinse or allow to air dry as directed\n\nThe contact time is the step most often skipped. A disinfectant wiped on and immediately wiped off has not worked. Most products need between 30 seconds and 5 minutes.\n\nUse products that meet BS EN 1276 or BS EN 13697. These standards show the product has been tested to kill bacteria under realistic conditions.\n\nSurfaces needing frequent disinfection include chopping boards, work surfaces, equipment handles, taps, door handles and anything touched between raw and ready to eat work.\n\nKeep a written cleaning schedule setting out what is cleaned, how, with what, how often and by whom. Deep cleaning of extraction, behind equipment and inside chillers should be scheduled rather than left until it is noticed.\n\nStore chemicals away from food and never in unlabelled containers.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "cooking_temperatures.txt": "Cooking temperatures\n\nThe UK does not set one single legal cooking temperature. The FSA standard control is 70 degrees Celsius held for 2 minutes at the centre of the food.\n\nSeveral time and temperature combinations achieve the same reduction in bacteria, and any of them is acceptable:\n\n60 degrees for 45 minutes\n65 degrees for 10 minutes\n70 degrees for 2 minutes\n75 degrees for 30 seconds\n80 degrees for 6 seconds\n\nThe time matters as much as the temperature. Reaching 70 degrees for an instant is not the same as holding it for two minutes.\n\nMeasure at the thickest part or the centre of the food, which is the last place to reach temperature. For a whole joint or a large tray, take several readings in different places.\n\nClean and disinfect the probe before and after each use, and allow the reading to settle before recording it.\n\nVisual checks support the temperature check but do not replace it. Juices running clear and no pink meat are useful signs, but colour is an unreliable guide on its own, particularly in minced products.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "cooling_food.txt": "Cooling cooked food\n\nCooked food that is not going to be served hot must be cooled quickly. UK guidance is to cool food to 8 degrees Celsius or below within 90 minutes.\n\nFood cools through the danger zone on its way down, so the longer it takes, the more bacteria can grow. Large volumes of dense food such as stews, rice, sauces and whole joints cool very slowly if left as they are.\n\nMethods that speed cooling:\n\nDivide food into smaller or shallower portions\nUse wide shallow trays rather than deep containers\nStir liquids during cooling\nUse a blast chiller where one is available\nStand containers in cold or iced water\nSeparate large joints into smaller pieces before chilling\n\nDo not put hot food directly into a fridge in large quantities. It raises the temperature of everything else in the unit and puts other stock at risk.\n\nOnce cooled, cover, label with the date, and refrigerate at 8 degrees or below.\n\nCooked rice deserves particular care. Bacillus cereus spores survive cooking and germinate at room temperature, so rice must be cooled fast and never left standing warm.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "cross_contamination.txt": "Cross contamination control\n\nCross contamination is the transfer of harmful bacteria from one food, surface or person to another. Raw meat, poultry, eggs, unwashed vegetables and shellfish are the usual sources.\n\nSeparation controls:\n\nStore raw meat and poultry below ready to eat food, so drips cannot fall onto it\nUse separate chillers for raw and ready to eat food where possible\nUse colour coded boards and knives, and keep them apart during washing up\nKeep raw and ready to eat preparation in different areas, or at different times with a full clean between\n\nHand contact is the most common route. Wash hands after handling raw food, after touching bins or packaging, after using the toilet, and before handling ready to eat food. Gloves are not a substitute; contaminated gloves spread bacteria exactly as contaminated hands do.\n\nCloths and equipment carry bacteria across a kitchen. Use disposable cloths, or launder reusable ones at high temperature. Disinfect probes, slicers, mixers and vacuum packers between raw and ready to eat use.\n\nPackaging and delivery crates come from outside and should not be placed on food preparation surfaces.\n\nWash fruit and vegetables that will be eaten raw, and keep soil covered produce away from ready to eat items.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "date_labels.txt": "Use by and best before dates\n\nThe two date marks mean different things and are often confused.\n\nUse by is a safety date. It appears on perishable foods such as meat, fish, dairy and prepared salads. Food must not be used or sold after its use by date, even if it looks and smells fine. Selling food past its use by date is an offence.\n\nBest before is a quality date. It appears on foods such as dried goods, tinned products and biscuits. After this date the product may lose flavour or texture, but it is not unsafe, and it may still legally be sold provided it is fit for consumption.\n\nOnce a product is opened, the use by date on the pack no longer applies. Opened products need their own secondary shelf life, recorded on a label with the date opened and the date it must be used by.\n\nFood prepared on site also needs a date label. Many kitchens apply a two day or three day rule from preparation for chilled cooked items, but the shelf life must be justified for the product rather than copied from another business.\n\nStock rotation is first in, first out. Put new stock behind old stock, and check dates during opening checks rather than when a dish is being plated.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "deliveries.txt": "Deliveries and goods in checks\n\nFood safety starts at the back door. Check every delivery before accepting it, and record the checks.\n\nWhat to check:\n\nTemperature of chilled items, which should be 8 degrees or below, and frozen items at minus 18 degrees or below\nDate codes, rejecting anything already past its use by date or too close to it\nPackaging condition, rejecting split, crushed, dented, rusted or damp packaging\nSigns of pests in or on the packaging and vehicle\nCleanliness of the delivery vehicle, and whether raw and ready to eat foods were separated in transit\nThat the product matches what was ordered, which matters for allergen management\n\nUse a clean probe between packs rather than piercing product where possible, or use an infrared thermometer for surface checks.\n\nPut deliveries away immediately. Chilled and frozen goods left on a loading bay or kitchen floor begin warming at once.\n\nRecord the supplier, the date, what was checked and anything rejected. This record also supports traceability, so that if a product is recalled you can identify what you received and where it went.\n\nBuy only from reputable suppliers who can provide product specifications, including allergen information, and keep those specifications on file.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "food_hygiene_rating.txt": "Food hygiene rating scheme\n\nThe Food Hygiene Rating Scheme gives businesses a score from 0 to 5 following an inspection by the local authority. The scheme is run by the FSA with local councils.\n\nThe ratings mean:\n\n5 is very good\n4 is good\n3 is generally satisfactory\n2 means improvement necessary\n1 means major improvement necessary\n0 means urgent improvement necessary\n\nThe score is based on three elements assessed at the inspection: how hygienically the food is handled, the physical condition of the premises, and how well the business manages food safety, including records and the food safety management system.\n\nThe third element carries significant weight. A clean kitchen with no records and no working management system will not score well.\n\nDisplaying the rating is mandatory in Wales and Northern Ireland. In England display is voluntary, although the rating is published online regardless.\n\nInspection frequency depends on the risk the business presents and its previous score. A business rated 5 is visited less often than one rated 1.\n\nA business can request a re-inspection once improvements are made, and can appeal a rating it believes is wrong. There is also a right to reply published alongside the rating.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "fourteen_allergens.txt": "The 14 allergens\n\nUK law requires 14 allergens to be declared. They are:\n\nCelery, including celeriac and celery salt\nCereals containing gluten, which means wheat, rye, barley and oats\nCrustaceans, such as prawns, crab and lobster\nEggs\nFish\nLupin, found in some flours and bakery goods\nMilk, including butter, cheese, cream and milk powder\nMolluscs, such as mussels, oysters, squid and snails\nMustard, including mustard powder and seeds\nPeanuts\nSesame seeds\nSoybeans\nSulphur dioxide and sulphites, where above 10mg per kg or litre\nTree nuts, meaning almonds, hazelnuts, walnuts, cashews, pecans, Brazil nuts, pistachios and macadamias\n\nPeanuts and tree nuts are separate categories. A peanut is a legume, not a nut, and someone allergic to one is not necessarily allergic to the other.\n\nSulphites appear in dried fruit, wine, some vinegars and some processed potato products, and are easily missed.\n\nThese 14 must be declared, but a customer may be allergic to something outside the list. Take any stated allergy seriously whether or not the ingredient appears here.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "frozen_storage.txt": "Freezing and frozen storage\n\nFrozen food must be stored at minus 18 degrees Celsius or below.\n\nFreezing stops bacterial growth but does not kill bacteria. When food is defrosted, any bacteria present become active again, so defrosted food must be treated as high risk and handled accordingly.\n\nDefrost food in a refrigerator rather than at room temperature, on the bottom shelf and in a covered container so that liquid cannot drip onto other food. Large items may need 24 hours or more.\n\nFood must be fully defrosted before cooking unless the product instructions state otherwise. A partly frozen centre will not reach cooking temperature in the expected time.\n\nDo not refreeze food that has been defrosted, unless it has been cooked in between. Cooked food made from defrosted raw ingredients can be frozen.\n\nLabel frozen stock with the date it went in and use it in rotation. Freezing does not improve quality, and food held too long develops freezer burn and off flavours even though it remains safe.\n\nIf a freezer fails, check whether food has begun to thaw. Food that has risen above minus 18 degrees but is still hard frozen can usually be used; food that has softened must be assessed and in most cases discarded.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "haccp_principles.txt": "HACCP principles\n\nHACCP stands for Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points. All food businesses in the UK are legally required to have a food safety management system based on HACCP principles.\n\nThe seven principles are:\n\nConduct a hazard analysis. Identify what could go wrong at each stage, covering biological hazards such as bacteria, chemical hazards such as cleaning products, and physical hazards such as glass or metal.\n\nDetermine the critical control points. These are the steps where a control is essential to prevent or eliminate a hazard. Cooking, chilling and hot holding are typical examples.\n\nEstablish critical limits. Set the measurable boundary at each control point, such as 70 degrees for 2 minutes, or 8 degrees or below.\n\nEstablish monitoring. Decide how each limit will be checked, how often, and by whom.\n\nEstablish corrective actions. Decide in advance what happens when a limit is breached, so staff act consistently rather than improvising.\n\nEstablish verification. Check that the system itself is working, through audits, equipment calibration and review of records.\n\nEstablish documentation and record keeping. Keep records proportionate to the size of the business.\n\nThe system must be reviewed whenever the menu, the process, the equipment or the premises change.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "hot_holding.txt": "Hot holding temperatures\n\nFood that is kept hot in order to control bacterial growth must be held at 63 degrees Celsius or above in England, Wales and Northern Ireland.\n\nThere is a limited exception for service and display. Food may be held below 63 degrees for a single period of up to two hours, after which it must be reheated to the correct temperature, chilled, or thrown away. The exception cannot be used repeatedly on the same batch.\n\nHot holding equipment is designed to maintain temperature, not to raise it. Cold or warm food placed into a hot cabinet will sit in the danger zone while it slowly heats, which is exactly the condition that allows bacteria to multiply. Food must reach its correct cooking temperature first, then move into hot holding.\n\nBring holding units up to temperature before any food goes in, and do not overfill them, as packed trays block airflow and leave cold spots.\n\nCheck hot held food with a clean probe thermometer rather than relying on the equipment thermostat. The thermostat reports the air temperature inside the unit, not the temperature at the centre of the food.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "personal_hygiene.txt": "Personal hygiene and handwashing\n\nAnyone handling food must maintain a high standard of personal cleanliness and wear suitable clean clothing.\n\nHandwashing method:\n\nWet hands with warm running water\nApply soap and rub all surfaces, including backs of hands, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and wrists\nContinue for at least 20 seconds\nRinse thoroughly\nDry with a disposable paper towel or hand dryer\n\nWash hands on entering the kitchen, after handling raw food, after using the toilet, after handling waste or packaging, after cleaning, after touching your face or hair, after a break, and before handling ready to eat food.\n\nUse a dedicated wash hand basin with hot and cold water, soap and drying facilities. Hands must not be washed in sinks used for food preparation or washing up.\n\nOther requirements:\n\nKeep nails short, clean and free of varnish and false nails\nRemove watches and jewellery, apart from a plain wedding band\nTie back and cover hair\nCover cuts and grazes with a brightly coloured waterproof dressing, usually blue so it is visible if it comes off\nDo not smoke, vape, eat or chew in food areas\nChange aprons and whites when they become soiled, and never wear kitchen whites outside the premises\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "pest_control.txt": "Pest control\n\nPremises must be kept free of pests. Rodents, insects and birds carry bacteria onto food and surfaces, and evidence of pests is one of the fastest routes to enforcement action.\n\nPreventive measures:\n\nKeep external doors closed, and fit brush strips and fly screens\nSeal gaps around pipework, cables and under doors\nStore food off the floor and away from walls\nKeep bin areas clean, with lids closed and bins away from doors\nClear spillages and food debris promptly, including behind and under equipment\nRemove cardboard packaging quickly, as it harbours insects and provides nesting material\n\nSigns to look for during opening and closing checks:\n\nDroppings, particularly along walls and in corners\nGnaw marks on packaging, pipework or doors\nSmear marks along rodent runs\nLive or dead insects, especially near drains and under equipment\nNesting material, shredded paper or packaging\nUnusual smells\n\nAct on any sign immediately and record it. Most businesses hold a contract with a pest control company that includes regular visits and a report, and those reports should be kept on file.\n\nDo not use domestic pesticides in a food area. Bait and treatment must be placed by a competent person and recorded on a site plan.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "reheating.txt": "Reheating food\n\nIn England, Wales and Northern Ireland, reheated food must reach 70 degrees Celsius for 2 minutes at the centre.\n\nIn Scotland the requirement is higher. Reheated food must reach 82 degrees Celsius, unless a lower temperature can be justified as safe.\n\nReheat food once only. Repeated cooling and reheating gives bacteria several opportunities to multiply, and each cycle passes through the danger zone twice.\n\nReheat in equipment designed for it, such as an oven, hob or microwave. Hot holding units, bains-marie and hot cupboards are not reheating equipment; they hold food at temperature and will leave it in the danger zone for hours while it slowly warms.\n\nWith a microwave, stir the food part way through and allow standing time, because microwave heating is uneven and cold spots are common.\n\nCheck the temperature at the centre with a clean probe before the food goes out. Once reheated, move the food straight into hot holding at 63 degrees or above, or serve it immediately.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "safer_food_better_business.txt": "Safer Food, Better Business\n\nSafer Food, Better Business, usually shortened to SFBB, is the FSA's food safety management pack for small businesses. It is a practical way to meet the HACCP requirement without writing a full HACCP plan.\n\nThe pack is built around four areas, known as the 4Cs:\n\nCleaning. Keeping surfaces, equipment and premises clean and disinfected.\nCooking. Making sure food is cooked and reheated thoroughly.\nChilling. Keeping cold food cold and cooling cooked food quickly.\nCross contamination. Stopping bacteria and allergens moving between foods.\n\nEach section sets out safe methods, and the business records that it is following them in a diary.\n\nThe diary records opening and closing checks, anything that went wrong, and what was done about it. Recording a problem and its correction is not an admission of failure; it is evidence that the system works.\n\nSFBB packs exist for caterers, retailers, childminders and several specific cuisines.\n\nThe pack is only useful if the safe methods reflect what the kitchen actually does. A pack filled in from the template without adjusting it to the real menu and equipment will not stand up to inspection, and more importantly will not catch real hazards.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "staff_illness.txt": "Staff illness and fitness to work\n\nFood handlers suffering from or carrying a disease that could be transmitted through food must not work in any food handling area.\n\nStaff must report before starting work:\n\nDiarrhoea or vomiting\nInfected wounds, skin infections, sores or boils\nDischarge from the ears, eyes, nose or mouth\nA diagnosis of a food poisoning organism\nContact with someone who has diarrhoea or vomiting, particularly within the household\n\nAnyong with diarrhoea or vomiting must stay away from food handling until 48 hours after symptoms have stopped completely. This is the single most important exclusion rule in a kitchen, because people are still shedding organisms after they feel better.\n\nSomeone returning to work should wash hands especially carefully and may be moved to non food duties initially.\n\nKeep a fitness to work record showing who reported what and what action was taken.\n\nReview the sick pay policy against the reporting policy. The FSA has warned that penalising staff for sickness absence discourages reporting, which is more dangerous than the absence itself. If staff believe reporting illness will cost them money, they will come to work ill and say nothing.\n\nReturning travellers who had diarrhoea abroad may need clearance before handling food.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "temperature_records.txt": "Temperature monitoring and records\n\nFood businesses must be able to show that temperature controls are working, which in practice means written records.\n\nTypical checks recorded daily:\n\nFridge and freezer temperatures, usually at the start and end of service\nDelivery temperatures as goods arrive\nCore temperatures of cooked food\nHot holding temperatures during service\nCooling checks on food being chilled down\n\nRecord the actual reading, not a tick. A column of ticks proves nothing to an inspector and hides a drifting unit from your own staff.\n\nUse a clean, calibrated probe thermometer for food temperatures. Check calibration regularly against iced water, which should read 0 degrees, and boiling water, which should read 100 degrees at sea level.\n\nRecord what was done when a reading was out of range. The corrective action is the part that matters: moving stock to another unit, discarding food, calling an engineer. An out of range reading with no recorded action looks worse than no record at all.\n\nKeep records for the period set out in your food safety management system. Many businesses keep them for at least three months, and longer where a product has a long shelf life.\n\nDigital probes and automatic data loggers are acceptable, provided the data can be produced on request.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it.",
    "waste_management.txt": "Waste management\n\nFood waste and other refuse must not be allowed to build up in food handling areas, and must be removed as often as necessary.\n\nInternal controls:\n\nUse bins with close fitting lids, ideally foot operated so they are not touched by hand\nLine bins and change liners before they overflow\nKeep bins away from food preparation surfaces\nClean and disinfect bins regularly, inside and out\nWash hands after touching bins or waste, every time\n\nExternal controls:\n\nKeep the bin store clean and tidy, with lids closed\nSite bins away from doors and openings where possible\nArrange collections frequently enough that waste does not accumulate\nKeep the area free of standing water and spilled food, which attract pests\n\nUsed cooking oil must be collected by a registered waste carrier and cannot be poured down drains. Keep the waste transfer notes.\n\nBusinesses producing food waste are required to separate it for collection in much of the UK, with requirements varying between England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. Check the rules that apply in your area.\n\nKeep records of who collects your waste and under what licence. Waste passed to an unregistered carrier remains the producer's legal responsibility.\n\nSource: Food Standards Agency guidance, food.gov.uk. Summarised for reference; always check the current FSA page before relying on it."
}
